---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHero from "../../components/PageHero.astro";
import CompareTable from "../../components/CompareTable.astro";
import CtaBand from "../../components/CtaBand.astro";

const rows = [
  { label: "Lives inside the content itself", them: false, us: true },
  { label: "Survives copy-paste of text", them: false, us: true },
  { label: "Survives platforms that strip file metadata", them: false, us: true },
  { label: "Declares provenance to tools that check for it", them: true, us: false },
  { label: "Identifies a copy that carries no labels at all", them: false, us: true },
  { label: "Produces evidence usable against a copy", them: false, us: true },
];
---

<Base
  title="Kirpik vs Content Credentials and C2PA"
  description="C2PA Content Credentials travel beside content, not inside it, and text copied out of a page carries no metadata at all. Why provenance metadata and embedded watermarking are different instruments."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Technique explainer"
    title="Content Credentials vs embedded watermarking"
    lead="C2PA and Content Credentials are a serious standard doing a real job: declaring where a file came from and how it was edited. The catch for theft cases is one word. Credentials travel beside content. A watermark travels inside it."
  />

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow prose">
      <h2>What C2PA metadata is</h2>
      <p>
        The Coalition for Content Provenance and Authenticity (C2PA) defines a way to attach signed
        metadata, marketed as Content Credentials, to a file. The credential records who made the file
        and what edits it has been through, and software that understands the format can verify the
        signatures. It grew up in the photography and news world as an answer to "is this image what it
        claims to be", and for that disclosure job it is well designed.
      </p>
      <p>
        One app in Shopify's anti-theft category takes this route. Mintall Keep's listing, as recorded in
        July 2026, offers "C2PA Content Credentials" and NFT provenance with "no badges or watermarks",
        an accurate description of an approach that deliberately marks nothing.
      </p>

      <h2>Beside, not inside</h2>
      <p>
        Metadata is a passenger, not a tattoo. It sits in a container next to the content, and it stays
        with a copy only when the copying process carries containers across. Most of the ways store
        content actually gets taken do not:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Text has no container at all.</strong> Select a product description, press Ctrl+C, and
          the clipboard holds characters. Not the page's meta tags, not a manifest, nothing signed.
          Copied text arrives at its destination naked, every time, by the nature of the clipboard.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Scrapers keep what they want.</strong> A feed importer reading product JSON takes the
          description field. Any provenance living elsewhere in the page or file never makes the trip.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Platforms strip metadata routinely.</strong> Image pipelines at social networks,
          marketplaces and CDNs re-encode uploads, and file metadata is commonly discarded in the
          process, credentials included.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        None of this is a flaw in C2PA. The standard's own remit is provenance disclosure for content
        that carries its credentials. A thief's copy is precisely the content that does not.
      </p>

      <h2>Disclosure versus evidence</h2>
      <p>
        The deeper difference is what each instrument is for. A credential says: this content, which
        still holds its paperwork, came from here. It speaks about the honest copy. A watermark says:
        this content, wherever it went and whatever labels it lost, is mine, and here is the signal
        inside it that proves so. It speaks about the stolen copy. Provenance for trust, watermarking for
        theft. You can value both; they are not substitutes.
      </p>
      <p>
        Kirpik is built on the second model, for the content that has no metadata to lose. Its invisible
        algorithmic watermark is derived from your original text and lives in the copy itself, so
        copy-paste is not an escape hatch, it is the delivery mechanism. Detection reads your store's
        watermark code out of a suspect page or pasted sample, and a Chain of Proof certificate assembles
        fingerprints, dates and an Internet Archive timeline into evidence a host or marketplace can
        verify independently.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <CompareTable themLabel="Content Credentials" rows={rows} />
      <p class="tnote">
        The credentials column describes C2PA-style metadata attached alongside content, per the
        published standard's design; platform metadata stripping is general, documented behaviour of
        image re-encoding pipelines. Mintall Keep is quoted from its own Shopify App Store listing, July
        2026, and compared in full <a href="/compare/mintallkeep/">on its own page</a>. Trademarks belong
        to their owners. Not legal advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    heading="Paperwork stays behind. The watermark goes along."
    sub="Kirpik marks the words themselves, so the copy that lost every label still answers to you. Free to start."
  />
</Base>

<style>
  .tnote { margin-top: 1rem; font-size: 0.84rem; color: var(--ink-faint); line-height: 1.65; }
  .tnote a { color: var(--orange-deep); font-weight: 600; }
</style>
